  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Supreme Court",
      "case_id": "2017-15-1509",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "611459431731b013bc43c7c9f3ab9d6b063af7e97b77aabc87954f3c5f155715",
      "evidence_id": "ev-611459431731-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:03 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2017-15-1509",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "611459431731b013bc43c7c9f3ab9d6b063af7e97b77aabc87954f3c5f155715",
      "evidence_id": "ev-611459431731-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: What is the legal standard?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2017-15-1509",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "611459431731b013bc43c7c9f3ab9d6b063af7e97b77aabc87954f3c5f155715",
      "evidence_id": "ev-611459431731-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: And how do they do that? I mean, you know, it might be important in some instances; in some other instances, it wouldn't be. I mean, he lists five factors and then on -- you know, at the end of this appendix, he has about four or five more factors, and I guess he saw the people. Did he see the people?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. CROSS",
      "case_id": "2017-15-1509",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "611459431731b013bc43c7c9f3ab9d6b063af7e97b77aabc87954f3c5f155715",
      "evidence_id": "ev-611459431731-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CROSS: Because there's a great level for a need -- there is a great need for uniformity in this area. I mean, that's another consideration. There's a substantial need for uniformity. In bankruptcy -\u00ad"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2017-15-1509",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "611459431731b013bc43c7c9f3ab9d6b063af7e97b77aabc87954f3c5f155715",
      "evidence_id": "ev-611459431731-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Can I ask you a question of -- drawing on your experience as a practitioner? In this case, and I suppose in other cases where this comes up, what is at issue is whether a plan of reorganization is going to be confirmed or whether the debtor is going to be liquidated. And from the perspective of bankruptcy judges in your experience, what is the dynamic regarding that determination? Do they have a tendency to try to achieve one result or the other?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. GEYSER",
      "case_id": "2017-15-1509",
      "confidence": 0.96,
      "document_hash": "611459431731b013bc43c7c9f3ab9d6b063af7e97b77aabc87954f3c5f155715",
      "evidence_id": "ev-611459431731-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GEYSER: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: Petitioners' theory requires at least three appellate judges over at least two rounds of appellate review to devote extensive time and resources to recreating an entire evidentiary record and redoing a trial judge fact-intensive work."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2017-15-1509",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "611459431731b013bc43c7c9f3ab9d6b063af7e97b77aabc87954f3c5f155715",
      "evidence_id": "ev-611459431731-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Should we wait to see what the courts of appeals sort out on all this before we decide what the standard of review is?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Goodspeed",
      "case_id": "2017-15-1509",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "611459431731b013bc43c7c9f3ab9d6b063af7e97b77aabc87954f3c5f155715",
      "evidence_id": "ev-611459431731-0002-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. GOODSPEED: So that's correct, Justice Kagan. Two things: first, as a general matter as Respondents' counsel suggested, how closeness is defined is so close that you're not operating at arm's length."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2017-15-1509",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "611459431731b013bc43c7c9f3ab9d6b063af7e97b77aabc87954f3c5f155715",
      "evidence_id": "ev-611459431731-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Well, suppose that Dr. Rabkin and Ms. Bartlett had a relationship that was exactly like that of a married couple except that they hadn't gotten married. They lived together for a long time, they shared finances, they had children together. Would the transaction -- would -\u00ad would -- could Dr. Rabkin then not be an insider on the ground that the particular transaction was done at arm's length? Does that seem right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Gregory A. Cross",
      "case_id": "2017-15-1509",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "611459431731b013bc43c7c9f3ab9d6b063af7e97b77aabc87954f3c5f155715",
      "evidence_id": "ev-611459431731-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. CROSS: Mr. Chief Justice, may it please the Court: Justice Gorsuch, I'd like to come to your point. The test -- what we're solving for is who does and does not satisfy insider status under the Bankruptcy Code. That's the question that the Court should have applied de novo review to."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2017-15-1509",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "611459431731b013bc43c7c9f3ab9d6b063af7e97b77aabc87954f3c5f155715",
      "evidence_id": "ev-611459431731-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 69,
        "start": 69
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: And you think the test is wrong. And -- and we didn't take that question. That's on us."
    },
    {
